// _kat_xmlpath_parse_audit.nova — soundness audit KAT for std/data/xmlpath.nova
// (the XPath-like slash-path query layer over std/text/xml's xml_parse).
//
// xmlpath.nova itself does no parsing of its own -- every private helper
// (_xp_split_path, _xp_walk, _xp_node_tag/_children/_text, _xp_resolve) is a
// plain iterative `while` loop over already-built dict/list values; none of
// them call themselves or each other recursively. The ONLY parse function in
// the call graph reachable from xmlpath's public API (xp_first / xp_all /
// xp_count / xp_exists -> _xp_resolve -> xml_parse) is std/text/xml's
// xml_parse, which IS real recursive descent: _xml_parse_element and
// _xml_parse_children are MUTUALLY RECURSIVE, one native call-stack frame
// pair per XML nesting level.
//
// CLASS 1 (DEEP-NESTING RECURSION DoS) -- REAL, FIXED (highest severity).
//   Before this audit, that mutual recursion had NO depth limit: a
//   ~20000-deep "<a><a>...</a></a>" document -- trivially craftable by any
//   caller feeding untrusted XML into xp_first/xp_exists/etc. (e.g. an HTTP
//   request body) -- recursed the native call stack ~20000 levels deep.
//   That is a classic unbounded-recursion stack-overflow DoS (crash/UB).
//   FIX (std/text/xml.nova): thread a `depth` counter through
//   _xml_parse_element / _xml_parse_children (root == 1), capped at 1000.
//   Past the cap, an element's own tag/attrs are still parsed (the
//   well-formed prefix survives intact) but _xml_parse_children is never
//   invoked for it -- native recursion cannot grow past ~1000 frames no
//   matter how deep the input claims to be, and the truncated element is
//   flagged with attrs["error"] = "max nesting depth exceeded".
//
// CLASS 2 (MALFORMED ACCEPTANCE) -- REAL, FIXED.
//   Three malformed shapes were silently accepted as if well-formed, with NO
//   signal anywhere in the returned tree:
//     - mismatched closing tag: "<a><b>x</a>" -- the "</a>" closed <b> with
//       ZERO name comparison (any "</...>" closed whatever was currently
//       open, regardless of name).
//     - unclosed element: EOF reached mid-document with no closing tag at
//       all silently returned as if the element legitimately ended there.
//     - unterminated attribute quote: a missing closing quote silently
//       swallowed the REST OF THE ENTIRE DOCUMENT into one attribute value
//       (including what looked like following child elements) with no
//       signal that anything was wrong.
//   FIX: all three now set attrs["error"] on the affected node -- the same
//   established idiom xml_parse already used for "no root element" /
//   "expected element" / "unexpected closing tag" -- instead of silently
//   returning a tree that looks fully well-formed.
//
// CLASS 3 (PATHOLOGICAL BLOWUP / entity expansion) -- NOT APPLICABLE, verified.
//   xml_parse expands exactly 5 FIXED predefined entities (&lt; &gt; &amp;
//   &quot; &apos;), each to exactly 1 literal character, via a flat
//   if/else-if chain with no table, no recursion, and no notion of a
//   user-defined entity at all. <!DOCTYPE>/<!ENTITY> declarations are
//   skipped by _xml_skip_decl (bracket-depth-tracked, never parsed into an
//   expansion table), so any "&x;" that would reference such an entity is
//   left VERBATIM. There is no expansion engine for a billion-laughs-style
//   chain to abuse -- structurally impossible, not merely rate-limited.
//
// CLASS 4 (UNBOUNDED NUMBER/LENGTH) -- NOT APPLICABLE.
//   This grammar has no length-prefixed or count-prefixed construct at all
//   (no "repeat N times" field, no binary length header) -- every construct
//   is delimiter-scanned (a matching quote, "-->", "]]>", "?>", ">"). No
//   integer is ever parsed from untrusted input anywhere in this module, so
//   there is no site for an i64-overflow-style unbounded-count bug to exist.
//
// CLASS 5 (CHAR-AT O(n^2) PERF-DoS) -- REAL, FIXED, IN xmlpath.nova ITSELF.
//   _xp_split_path (xmlpath's OWN code, not xml.nova's) used to be
//   `while i < n: c = char_at(path, i); seg = seg + c; i = i + 1`. char_at()
//   decodes UTF-8 from byte offset 0 on EVERY call (nova_rt_char_at in
//   nova_runtime.c: `size_t i = 0; while (i < len) { ...; if (idx==index)
//   return ...; idx++ }` -- an O(index) rescan per call), so a single-pass
//   loop that calls it once per index over the WHOLE path is O(n^2) total --
//   the exact same bug class the ini.nova _ini_split_lines audit found and
//   fixed. The `path` argument is exactly the kind of value real callers
//   build from untrusted input (a URL segment, a query parameter used to
//   select an XML field) -- an attacker-controlled long path string makes
//   EVERY xmlpath public function (xp_first/xp_all/xp_count/xp_exists, all
//   of which route through _xp_resolve -> _xp_split_path) pathologically
//   slow, independent of the XML document size.
//   FIX (std/data/xmlpath.nova): rewritten to str_to_bytes/bytes_get (O(1)
//   random byte access) + buffer()/buf_append_char/buf_to_str (O(1)-
//   amortized append) -- true O(n). Copying raw bytes instead of char_at's
//   decoded codepoints still reconstructs multi-byte UTF-8 sequences
//   byte-for-byte, because "/" (0x2F) can never appear as a UTF-8
//   continuation or lead byte (those are always >= 0x80) -- PART F proves
//   both the perf fix (a ~40000-char adversarial path returns promptly) and
//   that multi-byte UTF-8 tag names in path segments still split correctly.
//
// All existing xmlpath.nova KAT vectors (see _xmlpath_test.nova) are
// preserved verbatim in PART A to prove the fix does not disturb any valid
// parse.

import std/data/xmlpath
import std/text/xml

// O(log count) doubling builder for the deep-nesting stress input below.
// NOTE: deliberately NOT std/core/str's str_repeat -- that helper builds via
// a naive one-at-a-time `out = out + s` loop, which is O(count) concat calls
// each re-copying the whole accumulated string (nova_fat_str_concat is
// O(la+lb) per call, see nova_runtime.c) -- O(count^2) total. Confirmed by
// direct measurement: str_repeat("<a>", 2000) is instant, str_repeat("<a>",
// 20000) alone blows the 15s test-runner timeout (10x the count, ~100x the
// quadratic cost). That is a pure test-harness performance concern in a
// DIFFERENT module (std/core/str.nova), not a parser soundness issue, so it
// is out of scope to fix here -- this local helper just avoids tripping over
// it while still exercising the full 20000-deep xml_parse recursion path.
fn build_repeat(unit: string, count: int) -> string
    let result = ""
    let piece = unit
    let remaining = count
    while remaining > 0
        if remaining - (remaining / 2) * 2 == 1
            result = result + piece
        piece = piece + piece
        remaining = remaining / 2
    result

fn cks(name: string, got: string, want: string)
    if got != want
        print("FAIL " + name + ": got='" + got + "' want='" + want + "'")
        exit(1)
    print("ok " + name)

fn cki(name: string, got: int, want: int)
    if got != want
        print("FAIL " + name + ": got=" + str(got) + " want=" + str(want))
        exit(1)
    print("ok " + name)

fn ckb(name: string, got: bool, want: bool)
    let gi = 0
    if got
        gi = 1
    let wi = 0
    if want
        wi = 1
    if gi != wi
        print("FAIL " + name + ": got=" + str(gi) + " want=" + str(wi))
        exit(1)
    print("ok " + name)

// ── PART A: valid-input round-trip (unchanged spec vectors) ───────────────
fn a_valid_roundtrip()
    cks("A first_simple", xp_first("<a><b>hi</b></a>", "a/b"), "hi")
    cks("A first_of_many", xp_first("<a><b>1</b><b>2</b></a>", "a/b"), "1")
    let all_b = xp_all("<a><b>1</b><b>2</b></a>", "a/b")
    cki("A all_len", len(all_b), 2)
    cks("A all_0", all_b[0], "1")
    cks("A all_1", all_b[1], "2")
    cki("A count_3", xp_count("<r><i>x</i><i>y</i><i>z</i></r>", "r/i"), 3)
    ckb("A exists_true", xp_exists("<a><b>x</b></a>", "a/b"), true)
    ckb("A exists_false", xp_exists("<a><b>x</b></a>", "a/c"), false)
    cks("A first_deep", xp_first("<a><b><c>deep</c></b></a>", "a/b/c"), "deep")

// ── PART B: CLASS 1 -- deep-nesting recursion DoS, MANDATORY case ─────────
fn b_class1_deep_nesting_capped()
    let deep_open = build_repeat("<a>", 20000)
    let deep_close = build_repeat("</a>", 20000)
    let deep_xml = deep_open + deep_close

    // Must return promptly (no crash, no hang) with a sane bounded result
    // through xmlpath's OWN public API -- this is what a real caller uses.
    ckb("B deep xp_exists(root)", xp_exists(deep_xml, "a"), true)
    cki("B deep xp_count(root)", xp_count(deep_xml, "a"), 1)
    cks("B deep xp_first(root) has no direct text", xp_first(deep_xml, "a"), "")

    // Prove the cap ACTUALLY fired (not "got lucky" surviving 20000 native
    // stack frames): parse directly and walk the first-child chain. An
    // uncapped parse would have genuine structural nesting 20000 deep;
    // capped, the chain must hit an explicit depth-cap error marker in well
    // under 2000 steps, proving real recursion never grew anywhere near
    // 20000 native frames.
    let root = xml_parse(deep_xml)
    cks("B deep root tag", root["tag"], "a")
    let node = root
    let steps = 0
    let hit_cap = false
    while steps < 2000 and len(node["children"]) > 0 and (not hit_cap)
        node = node["children"][0]
        steps = steps + 1
        if xml_attr(node, "error") == "max nesting depth exceeded"
            hit_cap = true
    ckb("B deep nesting cap fired", hit_cap, true)
    if steps >= 2000
        print("FAIL B deep nesting cap: did not fire within 2000 steps (unbounded recursion regression)")
        exit(1)
    print("ok B deep nesting cap fired at step " + str(steps) + " (bounded, not 20000)")

// ── PART C: CLASS 2 -- malformed acceptance, each case asserted rejected ──
fn c_class2_malformed_rejected()
    // C1: mismatched closing tag -- "</a>" used to silently close <b> with
    // zero name check. Now flagged, without disturbing the still
    // best-effort-recovered text extraction existing callers rely on.
    let mism = xml_parse("<a><b>hi</a>")
    let bnode = xml_find(mism, "b")
    cks("C1 mismatched close flagged on <b>", xml_attr(bnode, "error"), "mismatched closing tag")
    cks("C1 xp_first still recovers text", xp_first("<a><b>hi</a>", "a/b"), "hi")

    // C2: unclosed element -- EOF reached with no closing tag anywhere.
    let unclosed = xml_parse("<a><b>hi")
    cks("C2 unclosed root flagged", xml_attr(unclosed, "error"), "unclosed element")
    ckb("C2 xp_exists degrades safely", xp_exists("<a><b>hi", "a/b"), true)

    // C3: unterminated attribute quote -- previously swallowed the rest of
    // the document (including the apparent child elements) into one
    // attribute value with no signal at all that anything was wrong.
    let badattr = xml_parse("<a x=\"never closes><b>hi</b></a>")
    cks("C3 unterminated quote flagged", xml_attr(badattr, "error"), "unterminated tag")

    // C4: empty input -- pre-existing behavior, reverified unchanged.
    let empty = xml_parse("")
    cks("C4 empty input flagged", xml_attr(empty, "error"), "no root element")
    cks("C4 xp_first on empty xml", xp_first("", "a"), "")

    // C5: lone closing tag, no root at all -- pre-existing, reverified.
    let lone = xml_parse("</a>")
    cks("C5 lone closing tag flagged", xml_attr(lone, "error"), "unexpected closing tag")

    // C6: wrong root tag via xmlpath's own API -- must degrade to
    // empty/false/0, never crash or fabricate a match.
    cks("C6 wrong root xp_first", xp_first("<a><b>hi</b></a>", "x/b"), "")
    cki("C6 wrong root xp_count", xp_count("<a><b>hi</b></a>", "x/b"), 0)
    ckb("C6 wrong root xp_exists", xp_exists("<a><b>hi</b></a>", "x/b"), false)

// ── PART D: CLASS 3 -- pathological blowup / entity expansion ─────────────
fn d_class3_entity_expansion_not_applicable()
    // A DOCTYPE declaring a custom entity must NOT be expanded anywhere.
    let dt = xml_parse("<?xml?><!DOCTYPE r [<!ENTITY x 'boom'>]><r>&x;ok</r>")
    cks("D custom entity left verbatim", xml_text(dt), "&x;ok")
    // The classic billion-laughs SHAPE (one entity referencing another
    // entity many times) is likewise inert -- still just literal text,
    // because there is no expansion table to chain through in the first
    // place.
    let bomb = xml_parse("<!DOCTYPE r [<!ENTITY a 'x'><!ENTITY b '&a;&a;&a;&a;&a;&a;&a;&a;&a;&a;'>]><r>&b;</r>")
    cks("D chained entity ref left verbatim", xml_text(bomb), "&b;")

// ── PART E: CLASS 4 -- unbounded number/length ─────────────────────────────
// See header: this grammar has no length/count-prefixed construct at all
// (every scan is delimiter-bounded), so there is no site for an
// i64-overflow-style bug to exist in. No runtime case needed -- the
// architecture itself rules the class out.

// ── PART F: CLASS 5 -- char_at O(n^2) perf-DoS in _xp_split_path ──────────
fn f_class5_split_path_perf_and_utf8()
    // F1: adversarial long path (~40000 bytes, no matching tag anywhere) must
    // return promptly. Pre-fix this was O(n^2) via char_at's O(index) rescan
    // per call -- ~40000 segments over a ~40000-char path is on the order of
    // 10^9 char_at-internal steps, which reliably blows the test-runner's
    // timeout. Post-fix (byte-indexed, O(n)) this completes instantly.
    let long_path = build_repeat("z/", 20000) + "final"
    cks("F1 long adversarial path -> no match, no hang", xp_first("<a><b>hi</b></a>", long_path), "")
    cki("F1 long adversarial path count", xp_count("<a><b>hi</b></a>", long_path), 0)

    // F2: multi-byte UTF-8 in a path segment must still split and match
    // correctly under the byte-indexed rewrite -- "/" (0x2F) never collides
    // with a UTF-8 continuation/lead byte (always >= 0x80), so raw-byte
    // copying reconstructs the multi-byte tag name intact.
    cks("F2 utf8 tag segment splits+matches", xp_first("<café><x>hi</x></café>", "café/x"), "hi")

    // F3: leading/trailing/double slash still produce the same empty-segment
    // safe-no-match behavior as before the rewrite (regression check).
    cks("F3 leading slash no match", xp_first("<a><b>hi</b></a>", "/a/b"), "")
    cks("F3 trailing slash no match on b (segment after b is empty)", xp_first("<a><b>hi</b></a>", "a/b/"), "")
    cks("F3 double slash no match", xp_first("<a><b>hi</b></a>", "a//b"), "")
    print("ok F class5 split-path perf+utf8")

fn main()
    a_valid_roundtrip()
    b_class1_deep_nesting_capped()
    c_class2_malformed_rejected()
    d_class3_entity_expansion_not_applicable()
    f_class5_split_path_perf_and_utf8()
    print("XMLPATH-PARSE-AUDIT KAT PASS")
